        ("What is the time complexity of inserting a node at the head of a singly linked list?", ["O(n)", "O(log n)", "O(1)", "O(n^2)"], 2, "Only the new node's next pointer and the head pointer change, regardless of list length."),
        ("A singly linked list has only a head pointer. What is the time complexity of inserting at the end?", ["O(1)", "O(log n)", "O(n^2)", "O(n), because you must traverse to the last node"], 3, "Without a tail pointer, you have to walk the whole list to find the last node."),
        ("What does traversing a linked list mean?", ["Sorting the nodes by value", "Going through the list by following the links from one node to the next", "Copying every node into an array", "Deleting nodes one at a time"], 1, "Traversal follows each next pointer until the end of the list."),
        ("Which are the basic operations in the lesson for linked lists?", ["Traversal, remove a node, insert a node and sort", "Push, pop, peek and size", "Enqueue and dequeue only", "Compile, link and run"], 0, "The lesson lists traversal, remove a node, insert a node and sort as the basic linked list operations."),
        ("Why do linked list nodes not need to be shifted when other nodes are inserted?", ["Nodes are always stored in sorted order", "The list uses an index for every node", "Nodes are not laid out one right after another in memory", "Nodes are stored in the CPU cache"], 2, "Because nodes are not contiguous, an insert only changes next pointers."),
        ("Why are linked list operations usually harder to program than array operations?", ["Linked lists cannot be sorted", "Linked lists need a fixed size", "Programming languages have better built-in support for arrays", "Arrays use pointers and linked lists do not"], 2, "The lesson notes that linked list operations need more lines of code than similar array operations."),
        ("To find the lowest value in a singly linked list, what must you do?", ["Traverse the list and keep track of the smallest value seen", "Read only the head node", "Jump straight to the middle node", "Read only the last node"], 0, "The values are not sorted, so every node has to be checked once."),
        ("How do you find the size of a singly linked list?", ["Read the value stored in the head", "Count the pointers in the last node", "Traverse from the head, counting nodes until the end (None)", "Use the index of the first node"], 2, "There is no stored length unless you keep one, so you count while traversing."),
        ("How do you delete the head node of a singly linked list?", ["Set the head's next pointer to None", "Set the head to the head's next node", "Set the last node's next to the head", "Delete the second node"], 1, "Moving the head pointer forward removes the first node from the list."),
        ("In a doubly linked list, how do you remove a node that is in the middle?", ["Link its previous node to its next node, and its next node back to its previous node", "Only set its own next pointer to None", "Move it to the end of the list", "Delete the head instead"], 0, "Both neighbors must be updated so that forward and backward links stay correct."),
        ("When inserting a new node between nodes A and B in a singly linked list, which order of steps is safe?", ["Point A's next to the new node first, then the new node's next to B", "Point the new node's next to B, then point A's next to the new node", "Set B's next to None, then link the new node", "Delete A, then insert the new node"], 1, "If A is redirected first, the link to B and the rest of the list would be lost."),
        ("How can you tell you have reached the end when traversing a circular linked list?", ["The next pointer is None", "The data is zero", "The list size is one", "You arrive back at the node where you started"], 3, "There is no NULL at the end of a circular list, so you stop when you return to the start."),
        ("What extra memory does a doubly linked list need compared with a singly linked list?", ["A second copy of the data in every node", "An index in every node", "A previous pointer in every node", "A counter for the whole program"], 2, "The previous pointer in each node costs extra memory but allows backward traversal."),
        ("What happens to an array when its fixed memory space fills up, compared with a linked list?", ["The whole array must move to a larger memory space, but a linked list does not need to", "Nothing, arrays grow automatically without cost", "The linked list must move instead", "Both must be sorted"], 0, "Linked lists have no fixed size, so they do not need to be moved when they grow."),
